## The fast answer

Use an interface (or type alias) when you just need to describe the *shape* of some data. Reach for a class only when you genuinely need a runtime instance with state and behaviour.

```ts
// Interface — describes data
interface User {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  isActive: boolean;
}

// Class — encapsulates behaviour and internal state
class RateLimiter {
  #tokens = new Map<string, number>();
  constructor(private maxPerSecond: number) {}

  allow(key: string): boolean {
    const now = Date.now();
    const last = this.#tokens.get(key) ?? 0;
    if (now - last < 1000 / this.maxPerSecond) return false;
    this.#tokens.set(key, now);
    return true;
  }
}
```

## The "is this data or is this behaviour?" test

If your "class" is just a constructor that sets fields and doesn't have meaningful methods, it should be an interface and a plain object:

```ts
// 🚫 A class for the sake of it
class User {
  constructor(
    public id: string,
    public email: string,
  ) {}
}

const u = new User("1", "a@example.com");

// ✅ Just data
interface User {
  id: string;
  email: string;
}

const u: User = { id: "1", email: "a@example.com" };
```

## When a class earns its keep

- **Stateful** — accumulates internal state across calls (caches, rate limiters, queues, EventEmitters)
- **Lifecycle** — connect/disconnect, open/close, start/stop
- **Encapsulation** — private fields that shouldn't be touched by consumers
- **Polymorphism** — multiple implementations of the same shape, swapped at runtime

```ts
abstract class Notifier {
  abstract send(message: string): Promise<void>;
}

class SlackNotifier extends Notifier {
  async send(message: string) { /* … */ }
}

class EmailNotifier extends Notifier {
  async send(message: string) { /* … */ }
}
```

If your codebase doesn't have those signals, interfaces and plain functions are simpler, more testable, and play better with serialisation. In React land that covers the vast majority of files.

detailMdTr:
## Hızlı Cevap

Yalnızca verinin *şeklini* tanımlaman gerekiyorsa interface (veya type alias) kullan. Durum ve davranışa sahip gerçek bir runtime instance'a ihtiyacın varsa class'a uzan.

## Interface Ne Zaman Kullanılır

```ts
// Component prop'ları
interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
  disabled?: boolean;
}

// API yanıt şekli
interface ApiUser {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  createdAt: string;
}

// Fonksiyon sözleşmesi
interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;
  error(message: string, error?: unknown): void;
}
```

Hiçbir runtime kodu yok — yalnızca compiler için şekil bilgisi.

## Class Ne Zaman Kullanılır

```ts
// Bağımlılıkları ve durumu olan servis
class UserService {
  constructor(private db: Database, private cache: Cache) {}

  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const cached = await this.cache.get(id);
    if (cached) return cached;
    return this.db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  }
}

// Method'ları olan model
class Money {
  constructor(private amount: number, private currency: string) {}

  add(other: Money): Money {
    if (other.currency !== this.currency) throw new Error('Para birimi uyuşmuyor');
    return new Money(this.amount + other.amount, this.currency);
  }

  toString() {
    return `${this.amount} ${this.currency}`;
  }
}
```

## Pratik Kural

Frontend'de (React) class nadiren doğru cevaptır. Component state için hook kullan, veri şekilleri için interface kullan, mantık için fonksiyonlar/hook'lar kullan.

Backend'de (Node/NestJS) class'lar servisler, controller'lar ve repository'ler için daha yaygındır çünkü framework DI için `new`'e ihtiyaç duyar.

